dmirable job bringing this  iteration of spider-man to life bringing  more individuality to the role than most  had before him the strange thing about  this show is that it was immediately  overshadowed by a different spider-man  cartoon spider-man and his amazing  friends which premiered the same day  features more marvel heroes and a  stronger lead performance by dan  gilvazan the two series also feature  nearly identical animation styles and  character designs which can make for a  disorienting viewing experience  unfortunately for shorts this is a  series that many have chosen to forget  boy some days it hardly pays to be a  lovable superhero  spider-man's very first live-action  appearance was on the electric company a  1970s educational series that ran on pbs  this spider-man doesn't talk and never  appears out of costume but his simple  adventures and easily described actions  are the perfect vehicle for teaching  kids basic language and math skills the  show also has a lot of fun bringing the  comic book aesthetic to life with danny  segren often framed by comic book panels  and speaking with the aid of word  balloons it's a trick to get children to  read of course but it does so with  plenty of fun and personality  admittedly this is kind of an odd one in  1990 spider-man starred in a short psa  about child abuse that was distributed  to schools and libraries there's no  green goblin or mysterio here just a  father who hits his daughter and a group  of kids who turn to spider-man for help  spider-man tells them that violence is  never the answer and recommends a  hotline to call spider-man doesn't have  a whole lot to do here since the  spotlight is on the kids but he's well  portrayed by veteran voice actor greg  snagoff credited here as gregory snow  he's reminiscent of the 80s animated  spider-man and more importantly has a  warmth to him when interacting with  troubled children he comes off as an  adult you can trust which is both  perfect for this cartoon and exactly  what spider-man should be  given time and a spider-verse sequel or  two this spider-man of the future could  potentially move much higher on this  list after all spider-man 2099 aka  geneticist miguel o'hara has a cult  following all his own on top of that  he's voiced by the incredibly popular  oscar isaac of star wars and ex machina  fame the problem of course is that he's  barely in spider-man into the  spider-verse at all appearing only in  the film's after credits sequence it's  fun to see him reenact a famous meme  from the 60s spider-man show but it's an  inauspicious beginning for miguel we can  only hope that he'll have more of a  presence in spider-verse 2.  two big things are distracting about  2003's spider-man the new animated  series a more mature take on the comics  that aired on mtv of all places the  first is the animation which combines 2d  cartooning with 3d computer modeling in  an incredibly awkward way the second is  that every time neil patrick harris's  version of spider-man talks it's hard  not to imagine doogie hauser or barney  stinson under the red and blue suit  harris wasn't a bad choice for  spider-man at the time but it's  difficult to accept him as peter parker  today when his voice has become so  recognizable as a musical tv star out of  all the spider-man cartoons since the  60s this one has probably aged the worst  everybody loves miles morales the modern  teenage spider-man created by brian  michael bendis and sarah pikelli in 2011  for marvel's now defunct ultimate comics  line even when the ultimate line ended  miles and his supporting cast managed to  survive thanks to his popularity and the  unique flavor he adds to the spider-man  franchise unfortunately bringing him  into the 2017 spider-man animated series  just didn't work that well rather than  originating from an alternate dimension  this version of miles played by naji  jeter is nothing more than a school  friend of peter parker's who gains his  own powers and dons his own costume when  he first goes into action he's portrayed  as a sloppy and overly enthusiastic hero  who initially does a very bad job peter  has to act as a coach and mentor despite  being somewhere around miles age the  whole storyline feels shoehorned in and  robs miles morales of much of what makes  him such a compelling character  gwen stacy has been a part of  spider-man's story in the comics since  1965 but it's only recently she's gained  super powers of her own known to the  world as spider-woman and to her friends  as spider-gwen this web slinger has been  a fan favorite since she first donned  the white and pink suit in 2014. the  gwen stacy of the 2017 spider-man series  however suffers from a similar problem  as miles morales she's a school mate of  peters rather than the main spider  person of her own world unfortunately  this take on gwen seems less like her  own superhero and more like just another  spidey sidekick perhaps this show chose  to avoid introducing a multiverse  because into the spider verse was  already in production but both spider  gwen and miles morales feel like cheap  imitations of their spider-verse  incarnations as a result still veteran  voice actor laura bailey does her best  with the material she's  given while lego productions are known  for drawing heavily from their source  material 2015's avengers reassembled  actually came out before spider-man's  first mcu appearance in captain america  civil war with no tom holland's  performance to base lego peter parker  off of this plastic take on spidey is a  bit generic compared to his counterparts  that aren't made out of bricks  still ben diskin gives it his all in the  role as he continues to do in the role  of flash thompson in the 2017 spider-man  series once the mcu established their  own spider-man however the lego one had  to evolve a bit to keep up  cole howard is an experienced and  talented young voice actor and his work  in 2020's lego marvel avengers climate  conundrum certainly fits the character  of peter parker however it's fairly  clear that howard got the part because  his spider-man voice sounds very much  like tom hollands howard spider-man  comes off as a bit younger and a lot  sillier than holland's live-action  performance but the similarity is still  unmistakable for lego marvel avengers  which is basically the movies but  sillier and aimed at younger audiences  cole howard is doing exactly what's  needed for the part  we're ranking these three spider kids  from the disney junior series as a group  rather than individually because frankly  these versions just aren't quite  distinctive enough to warrant their own  entries spidey and his amazing friends  is aimed at preschool kids which means  all the sharp corners are pretty much  sanded off of course that doesn't mean  it's a bad show it's pleasant and  likable enough with each episode  featuring two short adventures in which  the spidey team faced similarly aged  down super villains and learn lessons  about sharing and so forth the villains  include a really great female doc ock  who's basically a little girl version of  catherine han from into the spider-verse  give it up doc ock you can't handle  spidey team  but can you handle the might of  gigantic squishy  as far as characterization goes however  there's just not much going on in a show  like this the spidey team is voiced by  actual kids and talented ones that which  does add to the show's unique energy as  kids shows go this one certainly could  be a lot worse but it's not where adult  fans are going in search of great takes  on spider-man and his friends  the very first spider-man introduced in  spider-man into the spider-verse is the  peter parker of miles morales earth  voiced by the absurdly charismatic chris  pine he's the big time hero of new york  and basically the perfect spider-man he  even has blonde hair unlike any other  version of peter parker to mark him as a  literal golden boy the problem however  is that a flawless spider-man doesn't  feel like a spider-man at all the whole  point of the character is that he's a  flawed hero and nothing ever seems to go  right for him this spider-man isn't in  the movie to be a character of his own  though he is there to provide an  idealized image that conflicts with the  inexperienced miles and the slevenly  peter b parker plus making him the  perfect superhero makes the tragedy of  his death that much greater especially  as he leaves behind a legacy that miles  must try and live up to  although the original spider-man noir  comic where this version of peter parker  originated is quite a serious affair the  version of him that appears into the  spider-verse is essentially a one note  joke character still thanks to plenty of  great lines and a flawless voice  performance by the peerless nicholas  cage he's an extremely funny joke  character sometimes i let matches burn  down to my fingertips just to feel  something anything  not only does spiderman noir sound like  a perfectly hard-boiled detective he  also looks extremely striking rendered  entirely in black and white halftone in  a colorful world if he comes back in  spider-verse 2 it might be fun to get to  know him a little better but even if we  don't we're still incredibly glad to  have seen him in action  the 1978 japanese spider-man live-action  tv series has developed a cult following  in the us even though it's never been  officially released here it was produced  by the toei company who later created  mighty morphin power rangers and they  infused spider-man with a similar  tokusatsu vibe shinjitoro plays  spider-man's face and voice while  hirofumi koga does much of the  performing inside the spider protector  suit in this series spider-man is a  motorcycle driver named takuya yamashiro  who gets his spider powers from a super  bracelet from the planet spider mostly  he does what american spider-man does  climbing walls spinning webs and so  forth being a true tokusatsu hero he  also has a race car and a giant robot  named leah pardon the show is a lot of  fun and toto and koga both do a great  job but it drifts pretty far from  spider-man as we understand him these  days its primary appeal is definitely  the camp factor  a far more streamlined and spidery mech  than leah pardon penny parker's spider  isn't nearly as big but it has a lot  more personality it's also co-piloted by  an actual spider that has a psychic link  with young penny played by kamiko glenn  together they're the spider heroes of a  new york in the year 3145 that exists  somewhere out in the spider-verse  penny and spider are inspired by  japanese mega anime like neon genesis  evangelion and gundam  although penny doesn't get too much  character development she's a great  example of just how far a spider hero  can get from the standard template we've  seen time and time again she also  provides some emotional punch in the  final battle as spider sacrifices  himself to save penny fortunately she's  able to save her spider friend from  inside the mech so hopefully they'll be  able to rebuild upon returning to their  own dimension  of all the secondary spider people in  spider-man into the spider-verse  spider-ham is by far the silliest that  works for him though in fact it's kind  of the whole point spider-ham is  essentially a looney tunes character  down to his silhouette resembling porky  pig he's even mastered cartoon physics  making him one of the most unpredictable  fighters the spider-verse has to offer  some kind of silly cartoon  spider-ham's on-screen appearance is a  departure from the original comic book  version of the character who starred in  1985's peter porker the spectacular  spider-ham there he's a little more  serious working alongside other animal  avengers like captain americat and iron  mouse in into the spiderverse however  the silliness of spiderham makes for  some of the movie's funniest moments  largely thanks to a fantastic voice  performance from comedian john mulaney  by 2017 it was pretty established in the  minds of fans what a teenage peter  parker should sound like and robbie  damond nails it aside from that however  not much about this version of the  character feels fresh or new the 2017  spider-man is a solid engaging animated  series but it's all kind of been done  already it's fun to see spider-man team  up with other marvel heroes like iron  man black widow and the hulk but story  lines featuring multiple web-slinging  characters lack the spark that made them  memorable in into the spider-verse  sometimes it feels like the most daring  part of this spider-man incarnation is  the way his belt doesn't quite fully  connect on the front of his costume  still if you're looking for a solid  spider-man voice performance you could  do a lot worse than robbie damond  back in the early days of 21st century  superhero movies years before the mcu  toby maguire got the job done he got in  great physical shape for sam raimi  spider-man and its sequels but kept the  charmingly goofy face that made peter  parker a relatable figure for audiences  of all ages while he never quite seems  like a kid the way an early career  spider-man should he's young looking  enough to sell a teenage performance  even though he was in his late 20s when  production began in the emotional scenes  maguire's always a little over the top  but he makes up for it with fun quips  and killer dance moves he's definitely a  solid spider-man but nothing to write  home about  ultimate spider-man was controversial  from day one for one thing it replaced  the popular spectacular spider-man  series which ended due to character  rights issues after disney purchased  marvel in 2009 some fans grew even more  irritated when drake bell of drake and  josh fame was hired to voice the title  character expecting that this version of  the character would be geared for a  younger less mature audience however if  you ignore any bad publicity bell is  actually a pretty great animated  spider-man he has a high capacity for  silliness and fun as spider-man always  should but he also comes through in the  darker moments that require a more  emotional peter parker although this  iteration of spider-man may not have  gotten the same rave reviews that  spectacular spider-man's peter parker  did bell certainly does the job  the biggest problem with andrew garfield  in the amazing spider-man is that he  always looked too old for the part  garfield may have made a good adult  peter parker if the script wrote him as  such but a nearly 30 year old man in the  role of a bullied highschooler is a hard  sell to the point that it almost never  stops being distracting even still  garfield's take on the wall crawler has  plenty going for him he looks good in  the suit his comedic timing is on point  and his chemistry with emma stone's gwen  stacy makes for one of the best romantic  storylines that spider-man media has to  offer garfield is a fine transitional  spider-man but not exactly the type that  would have worked best in the marvel  cinematic universe  for a lot of millennials christopher  daniel barnes was the definitive voice  of spider-man during their childhood  having played the role on fox kids from  1994 to 1998. while this spider-man  series has aged a little awkwardly it  was the first spider-man tv show to draw  on the comics story lines in a nuanced  way introducing characters like venom  and even the punisher to saturday  morning viewers this series saves the  origin story for the theme song and  begins with peter parker already in  college and freelancing for the daily  bugle within that context barnes is  perfect in the part balancing the jokes  and the emotional baggage that are both  so central to spider-man with its solid  characterization and season-long story  arcs this show is one of the best takes  on grown-up peter parker that you can  find  for those a little older dan gilvazhan  is the saturday morning spider-man of  choice he voices the wall-crawling hero  on spider-man and his amazing friends a  show that brought the marvel universe to  life from 1981 until 1983. gilvazan was  a mainstay of 1980s animation also known  for his roles as bumblebee and  transformers and cooler in pound puppies  his spider-man has the lightness that  earlier versions of the character had  been missing while still seeming more  grounded than his goofier friend iceman  played by frank welker this cartoon may  seem pretty silly by today's standards  but this was the first time spider-man  really felt like the spider-man we all  know today and that's worth something  despite failing to gain momentum in the  2017 cartoon spider gwen thankfully got  her chance to shine as one of the  numerous spider people featured in into  the spider verse as the one and only  spider hero of her world this  incarnation of gwen stacy was motivated  to protect new york city by the death of  her best friend peter parker before  getting sucked into an extra dimensional  wormhole despite rocky first impressions  gwen becomes a close friend to miles and  despite a hint of romantic tension  there's nothing too forced or mushy to  distract from daring heroics as far as  spider people go spider gwen is one of  the strongest while miles is new to the  game and peter b's glory days are behind  him gwen is in the prime of her  superhero career she's graceful strong  and always helpful in a pinch hayley  steinfeld gives a standout voice  performance as gwen with the perfect mix  of youthful enthusiasm and effortless  ability she's not exactly spider-man but  as far as spider people go she's one of  the best  peter parker has always been a bit of a  sad sack but peter b parker from into  the spider-verse takes it to a whole new  level as the hero of new york for over  two decades the years have taken their  toll on peter as he approaches 40 he  finds himself broke separated from his  wife mary jane and gaining a noticeable  punch he's the natural end point of  peter parker's bad luck and the exact  opposite of the spider-man that miles  morales grew up seeing however once he  realizes it's up to him to mentor young  miles in the art of web slinging he  rediscovers the hero he's always been  deep down this is a take on peter parker  that's never quite been seen before and  wouldn't work in most contexts but in  spider-man into the spider-verse he's  perfect hilarious and ultimately  touching jake johnson star of tv's new  girl is the perfect choice to voice this  version of the character and gives a  surprisingly nuanced performance he may  not be the best or the most powerful  spider-man but he might just be the most  endearing one  spectacular spider-man is considered by  many to be the perfect 21st century  spider-man cartoon and one that ended  far too soon for frustrating reasons it  ran for two seasons from 2008 to 2009  and was being considered for a third  when disney bought marvel and reacquired  the television rights to spider-man  leaving this sony produced series in  legal limbo unable to continue  spectacular spider-man takes peter  parker back to high school with a unique  new design aesthetic that fits perfectly  voice actor josh keaton plays this  youthful spider-man with the earnestness  of a kid trying to do his best and he  couldn't have done a better job it's  just a shame we couldn't have seen more  of him  despite being the third live-action  spidey inside of a decade tom holland's  take on peter parker was a fan favorite  from the moment the world first saw him  steal the vibranium shield in the  trailer for captain america civil war  unlike maguire and garfield however  holland has the authentic look and feel  of an anxious teenager who's got himself  way in over his head the genuine  youthfulness and naivete of holland's  portrayal of peter parker contrasts  perfectly with the adult heroes of the  marvel cinematic universe especially  iron man tony stark's mentorship of  peter adds serious emotional depth to  both characters in a way no other  version of spider-man has done while  spider-man is a character defined by  tragedy no other actors have captured  the raw emotion of peter parker quite as  effectively as holland has as we've  witnessed in one of the most emotionally  devastating moments the mcu has to offer  i don't want to go i don't want to go  start please please  as far as the solo efforts go holland's  peter parker is an awkward yet  charismatic young hero who's still  finding his place in the world trying to  balance the life of an honor student  with the life of a superhero even though  he's constantly having to clean up his  own mistakes wherever he goes he comes  across as inexperienced without ever  seeming incompetent and by the end of  far from home he's become a hero that  tony stark would be proud of all of this  is topped off with a technological  marvel of a costume which gives  spider-man the opportunity to be more  expressive than any other live-action  version of him had been before details  like this combined with tom holland's  stellar performance make for the best  peter parker outside of the comics  themselves  with apologies to all versions of peter  parker miles morales is the spider-man  of the future he's energetic he's  artistic and as the son of a black  father and a puerto rican mother he  represents the changing face of america  in the best possible light as someone  who grew up in spider-man's friendly  neighborhood miles finds himself with  some pretty big shoes to fill after the  death of his world's peter parker  however with some help from his family  and a half dozen other spider people  miles is able to figure out his  abilities and become the hero his city  needs it's more than just his background  that makes miles as spider-man unique  however his invisibility and venom  strike abilities eventually make him a  fearsome foe for baddies like kingpin  despite all the spider people who appear  throughout into the spider-verse it's  miles's story first and foremost and  just as it's the best spider-man movie  ever made it shows how miles has the  potential to be the best spider-man it  certainly helps that he's voiced by  shamiq moore from the get down who  embodies the character flawlessly it's  only a matter of time until we see miles  in live action whether he's replacing  peter parker or working alongside him  miles morales is the future of  spider-man and the future is bright  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite marvel characters are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]

